{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Warren1968-T4-HLHLHLHHLHLHLH-x2-aH100",
  "Source": "S. W. Warren, \"Properties and performance of basic designs of infrared interference filters,\" Infrared Phys. 8, 65-78 (1968). DOI not printed in the paper - Table 4, p. 73",
  "Notes": "Double half-wave bandpass (basic unit HLHLHLHHLHLHLHS repeated twice) with absorbing high-index (germanium) layers, alpha = 100 cm^-1; L non-absorbing; 27 layers\nSpecification: Peak transmission at lambda0 = 4 um with absorbing Ge (H) layers.\nReported: Table 4 (p. 73): T at lambda0/lambda = 1, lambda0 = 4 um, alpha_H = 100 cm^-1: 16.2 % (lossless 84.6 %, alpha_H 100: 16.2 %, 200: 4.18 %).\nNotes: Design string written out: HLHLHLHHLHLHLHLHLHLHLHHLHLHLHS. Table 4 title says double half wave design; rows 3-4 (HLHLLHLHS, HLHLHHLHLHS) lack the asterisk, but all are taken as doubled (they share the lossless 84.6 %). alpha is taken as the intensity absorption coefficient, k = alpha*lambda/(4 pi) at 4 um (100 cm^-1 -> k = 0.003183, 200 -> 0.006366), applied as a constant k. Check: recomputation gives 83.5/82.4 % (HLLHS*), 73.86 % (HLHHLHS*, 200), 60.3/42.7 % (HLHLHHLHLHS, 100/200). H = quarter-wave optical thickness of index 4.0, L = quarter wave of index 1.8 at lambda0, S = substrate of index 1.65 (p. 66); adjacent equal letters (HH, LL) are merged into one half-wave layer of 2 QWOT. The paper writes designs from the incident (air) side to S, so the order is kept as printed. lambda0 is not fixed for Tables 1-3 and the figures (results are in lambda0/lambda); 4000 nm (4 um, as in Tables 4-6) is used, which is harmless because all indices are constant. Recomputations against the paper match only if the uncoated back surface of the n=1.65 substrate is included incoherently (substrate -> air): air/L/1.65 alone gives T = 89.4 % at lambda0 while Table 4 prints 84.6 %, and with the back surface it is 84.6 %; the rejection minima of Sec. 2.1.3 (3.45 %, 0.087 %, 0.0020 %, 0.000047 %) also match exactly only with the back surface. Normal incidence, unpolarised.\nLayers from Table 4, p. 73; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours T 16.2 % at 4 um; paper T 16.2 % (Table 4).",
  "Wavelength": [
    4000
  ],
  "Materials": [
    {
      "Name": "H n=4.0 alpha=100/cm (Warren 1968)",
